  "prompt_text": "The figure contrasts ordinary nuclear matter with matter that contains a strange quark. At the top, simple cartoon models show the quark content of four baryons. A neutron and proton are built from combinations of up and down quarks, shown in two colors. In contrast, the Λ and Ξ hyperons include one or more strange quarks, highlighted in a third color. The labels and colored dots emphasize that hyperons are similar three-quark particles but differ by replacing one or two light quarks with strange quarks.\n\nThe lower part of the image extends this idea to nuclei. On the left, an ordinary nucleus is depicted as a cluster of nucleons in two colors, representing protons and neutrons. On the right, a “single Λ-hypernucleus” looks similar except that one constituent is replaced by a green particle, indicating an embedded Λ hyperon inside the nucleus.\n\nOverall, the figure illustrates how hypernuclei are formed by introducing a strange-quark-containing baryon into otherwise ordinary nuclear matter. The visual comparison highlights the transition from familiar proton-neutron nuclei to nuclei that contain strangeness.",
